In article <1992Jun9.173632.4364@sei.cmu.edu> poh@sei.cmu.edu (Peter Hill) 
writes:

>1. Does killing creatures of the same alignment as you (or ones that don't 
>attack first) anger your god?

Not really.  You do have a 50% chance of losing one point of luck
every time you attack a peaceful monster, but your god usually doesn't
care.  The exception to this is unicorns.  Killing a peaceful unicorn
will drop your luck by 5 points.  Sacricifing a unicorn on a co-aligned
altar *will* anger the gods.  Other than that, don't worry about it.

>2. Whats a good strategy for getting enough to eat?  I've had some good 
>characters that end up starving to death or being killed while fainting from
>hunger. Should I just eat everything I kill?

It's no coincidence that the symbol for a monster corpse is the same
as the one for food.  You should eat them quickly, though, as they
spoil fast and spoiled meat is very unhealthy.  Also, not all monsters
are good to eat.  Some are obvious (cockatrices, humans, the Medusa),
but others aren't.  Trial and error is usually the only way.

>3. Do potions of the same color always do the; same thing?  And along the same
>lines how about rods and wands of the same material?

Only within the context of that game.  Once you start a new game, the
item descriptions are re-shuffled.  The one exception to that is the
potion of water which is always clear.

>4. Is there any where I can find a comparison list of the amount of damage
>weapons do?

Yes.  Check the Wheaton spoiler file.  It should be mentioned in the FAQ.

>5.  Does your kitten or dog gain experience or get stronger at the same rate
>you do?

Well, sorta.  Your pet gains one hit point for every monster it kills.
After a certain number of hp (8?), it gains an extra hit die.
The maximum number of hp (I think) is 85.

>6. Finally, wich classes *shouldn't* bother to try to learn spells?  Is it
>only the wizard that can learn and cast spells effectively?

Any class of character can cast spells, provided their Int is high
enough, but I usually don't bother learning spells with any character
until I've discovered a scroll of amnesia.  Once I've discovered that,
I know I can test scrolls without losing any spells I've memorized.

>Peter

